From d8b79cabd1dccca71380b3eb82d2a19be8ee82d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 00:53:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] services: a phone remote control in one step, and where to
 point the app

The remote control apps for phones (Kore, Yatse and the like) need the web
server of the media centre with a user and a password, and control from
applications on other devices; both are off by default, in different
places, and the app then asks for an address that the system does not
show anywhere easy to find.

Services > "Phone remote control" > "Set up a phone app (Kore, Yatse)":
- if all of it is on already, it shows the addresses of the system on the
  network, the port, the user and the password to enter in the app;
- if not, it says what turning it on means (whoever has the password and
  is on the network can control the media centre, its files and its
  add-ons, which here can do anything on the system), asks with "no"
  preselected, and turns it on through the media centre's own settings
  (JSON-RPC): a user and a password first (a password of 12 letters and
  digits is made when there is none; one that there is is kept), then the
  web server with authentication and the control from other devices. The
  media centre asks its own two confirmations; what it refuses stays as it
  was, and then it says that it was not turned on. Then the details;
- with the firewall in "Public", which lets nothing in, it says that the
  phone will reach the system only in "Home".

Nothing else changes: the settings are the media centre's and stay
there, off by default, and can be changed or turned off where they always
were (Settings > Services > Control). Texts in English and the three
Spanish of Hades.

Tested without Kodi, with a stand-in for its JSON-RPC that keeps the rules
of NetworkServices.cpp (no web server with authentication and no
password, and its two confirmations): 30 checks, and each of 9 changes
that take out a part of it makes the test fail. Not run in Kodi.
